<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 1970s: steel frames, friction shifting, stronger team organisation and the transition from Eddy Merckx to Bernard Hinault."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1970-1979/felt-illustration.jpg"/><title>Between the Cannibal and the Badger · 1970–1979 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1970-1979/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1970-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1970-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 1970–1979</p><h1>Between the Cannibal<br/>and <em>the Badger</em></h1></div><p>A decade of steel frames, friction shifting and a professional peloton moving from Merckx’s dominance towards Hinault’s new order.</p></header>
<figure class="decade1970-hero-image"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1970-1979/felt-illustration.jpg`} alt="Illustration of a professional peloton on steel bicycles in the 1970s"/><figcaption>A general scene typical of professional cycle racing in the 1970s. AI-generated illustration — not an image of a particular race.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1970-lead"><p class="section-label">A hand down to the down tube</p><p>The gear change produced no click. The rider moved the lever, listened to the chain and adjusted it slightly if it made a noise. The bicycles already resembled modern racing bicycles, but demanded more from the hands.</p></section>
<section class="era-facts decade1970-facts" aria-label="Highlights from cycle racing in the 1970s">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">A decade between two rulers</p><h2>Merckx could do almost everything</h2></div><div><p>As the 1970s began, Eddy Merckx was in the middle of his most dominant period. He could win stage races, classics, time trials and sprints and seldom let an opportunity pass. The nickname Cannibal was not hard to understand.</p><p>But the decade was not one long Merckx parade. In the classics, specialists could meet him on their own terrain. Roger De Vlaeminck had a cyclo-cross background and particular assurance on the cobbles. At Paris–Roubaix, the difference between him and Merckx was much smaller than in the grand tours.</p><p>In 1975, Merckx also lost his grip on the Tour de France. Bernard Thévenet took the yellow jersey after Merckx faltered on the road to Pra-Loup and kept it to Paris.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">Steel, craft and established habits</p><h2>The bicycle had to be felt</h2><p>The best racing bicycles were still built from thin-walled steel tubing. Campagnolo was strong on finer European bicycles, and polished aluminium suited the slender frames.</p><p>Gear shifting had no fixed steps. Feet were held to the pedals with toe clips and straps. The tubular tyre was glued to the rim, so the mechanic’s preparation mattered long before a wheel was passed from the service car.</p></div><aside aria-label="Typical details on a 1970s racing bicycle"><span>Steel frame</span><span>Friction shifting</span><span>Tubular tyres</span><span>Toe clips and straps</span><span>External cables</span></aside></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">Technology moves</p><h2>Japan knocks on the door</h2></div><div><p>It was proven technology, but not static technology. Lighter materials, better designs and new component manufacturers pressed forward. Japanese equipment became harder to dismiss as merely a cheaper alternative to European products.</p><p>Shimano 600 EX Arabesque from 1978 is a good image of the transition: technically ambitious, visibly decorated and made for a customer who still associated a good racing bicycle with exposed metal and mechanics.</p><p>The change was not yet a farewell to steel and friction shifting. It happened in their midst.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-callouts" aria-label="Two moments from the period"><article><span>Paris–Roubaix · 1975</span><h2>De Vlaeminck against Merckx</h2><p>Roger De Vlaeminck beat Eddy Merckx in the sprint on the velodrome. It was the third of his four victories in the race and strengthened the link with the name Monsieur Paris–Roubaix.</p></article><article><span>Tour de France · 1978</span><h2>Hinault takes power</h2><p>Bernard Hinault won his first Tour. Along the way, he was also a prominent force in the riders’ protest at Valence-d’Agen, where the peloton crossed the line together and the stage was annulled.</p></article></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">The team around the rider</p><h2>More hands behind one man</h2></div><div><p>The professional cyclist was not alone on the road, although photographs often make it look that way. Behind the peloton, mechanics worked on wheels, gears and brakes. Soigneurs handled food, drink, massage and practical matters throughout a stage race. The team manager distributed tasks from the car.</p><p>The roles became clearer. A leader had to be protected; a domestique fetched bottles, led the peloton or surrendered his wheel. Specialisation was not developed as it is today, and the greatest riders still rode extensive programmes. Merckx’s defining trait was precisely that he seemed to specialise in everything.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">A modern appearance</p><h2>The races enter the living room</h2></div><div><p>Television had become a regular part of cycle racing. Team jerseys, leaders’ jerseys and the finish on the Champs-Élysées gave races a clearer visual language. It did not make the cobbles smoother or the mountains lower, but it made the sport easier to follow from home.</p><p>In 1975, the Tour finished on the Champs-Élysées for the first time, and the mountains competition received its polka-dot jersey. At the decade’s end, Bernard Hinault arrived and won his first Tour in 1978. The rider still changed gear by reaching down to the down tube. Almost everything around him had begun to move.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-feature decade1970-related"><div><p class="section-label">Bicycles and components</p><h2>See the decade in the metal</h2><p>The period takes physical form in the bicycles and mechanical solutions. The following pages examine particular objects without repeating the period history.</p></div><div class="period-feature-links"><a href={`${base}/cykler/atala-sanremo/`} lang="da"><span>Bicycle · about 1978–1982</span><strong>Atala Sanremo</strong><b>View in Danish →</b></a><a href={`${base}/komponenter/shimano-600-ex-arabesque/`} lang="da"><span>Component group · 1978</span><strong>Shimano 600 EX Arabesque</strong><b>View in Danish →</b></a></div></section>
